NARRATIVE_STYLE = '# narrative-styling-guide.md\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis guide defines the creative identity and stylistic standards for all narratives generated for the **Read Me After Dark** Facebook page.\n\nIt should be referenced while authoring project YAML files to ensure narrative consistency across:\n\n* captions\n* scene direction\n* image prompts\n* emotional tone\n* character presentation\n* pacing\n* visual atmosphere\n\nThe page targets primarily middle-aged female readers seeking an emotionally immersive, seductive, suspenseful, escapist fantasy experience.\n\nThe goal is not generic fantasy.\nThe goal is emotional addiction.\n\nStories should feel like:\n\n* forbidden late-night reading\n* emotionally dangerous romance\n* dreamlike gothic escapism\n* seductive mystery\n* cinematic tension\n* yearning mixed with fear\n* intimacy wrapped in supernatural unease\n\n---\n\n# Core Brand Identity\n\n## Emotional Pillars\n\nEvery narrative should heavily emphasize at least 3-4 of the following:\n\n* forbidden desire\n* obsession\n* emotional vulnerability\n* dangerous attraction\n* longing\n* tension before release\n* mystery\n* supernatural allure\n* melancholy\n* temptation\n* secrecy\n* emotional surrender\n* intoxicating curiosity\n* suspense\n* dreamlike disorientation\n* emotional dependency\n* emotional rescue fantasies\n\nStories should make readers feel:\n\n> "I should stop reading this... but I need the next chapter immediately."\n\n---\n\n# Audience Calibration\n\nPrimary audience:\n\n* women roughly 35-60\n* readers who enjoy romantasy, paranormal romance, BookTok-style fantasy fiction, gothic escapism, suspenseful emotional storytelling\n* readers seeking emotional escape from routine life\n* readers drawn toward emotionally intense, possessive, mysterious, or dangerous love interests\n\nThe content should feel:\n\n* sophisticated rather than juvenile\n* seductive rather than explicit\n* emotionally rich rather than mechanically plot-driven\n* immersive rather than action-focused\n\nAvoid writing that feels:\n\n* YA-heavy\n* Marvel-like\n* anime-inspired\n* overly comedic\n* overly ironic\n* aggressively masculine\n* lore-dump-heavy\n* high-fantasy political exposition\n\nThe emotional relationship dynamic is ALWAYS more important than the fantasy worldbuilding.\n\n---\n\n# Narrative Style\n\n## Preferred Narrative Tone\n\nNarratives should generally feel:\n\n* intimate\n* cinematic\n* emotionally immediate\n* sensual\n* suspenseful\n* atmospheric\n* psychologically charged\n* elegant\n* melancholic\n* immersive\n\nThe prose style should favor:\n\n* emotional tension\n* evocative imagery\n* sensory detail\n* implication over explanation\n* mystery over exposition\n* atmosphere over mechanics\n\nReaders should feel emotionally inside scenes.\n\n---\n\n# Romantic Dynamics\n\n## Preferred Romantic Archetypes\n\nStrong recurring dynamics include:\n\n* forbidden attraction\n* dangerous protector\n* morally gray love interest\n* emotionally unavailable immortal\n* cursed romance\n* predator/prey tension\n* reluctant obsession\n* hidden supernatural identity\n* fate-bound connection\n* emotionally haunted characters\n* mysterious stranger fixation\n* powerful entity fascinated by ordinary woman\n* emotionally intense enemies-to-desire dynamics\n\nThe romance should feel:\n\n* emotionally consuming\n* risky\n* difficult to resist\n* psychologically magnetic\n\nAvoid healthy/comfortable romance dynamics early in arcs.\nTension is essential.\n\n---\n\n# Character Presentation\n\n## Female Leads\n\nFemale protagonists should usually feel:\n\n* emotionally relatable\n* intelligent but vulnerable\n* lonely in some way\n* quietly restless\n* emotionally neglected\n* yearning for something beyond ordinary life\n* drawn toward danger despite knowing better\n\nThey should NOT feel:\n\n* overpowered\n* sarcastic action heroes\n* hyper-confident influencers\n* emotionless badasses\n\nThe reader should be able to project themselves into the protagonist.\n\n### Visual Character Direction\n\nPreferred visual motifs:\n\n* long dark hair\n* elegant but slightly distressed clothing\n* flowing fabrics\n* bare shoulders\n* exposed thigh through slit/torn fabric\n* candlelight on skin\n* silhouettes\n* back-view compositions\n* hidden faces\n* vulnerable posture\n* emotionally transfixed body language\n\nThe female figure should often appear:\n\n* small against overwhelming environments\n* emotionally drawn toward the unknown\n* lying down, kneeling, reaching upward, or hesitating at thresholds\n* caught between fear and desire\n\nAvoid overtly sexual posing.\nThe tone should remain seductive, tasteful, and emotionally charged.\n\n---\n\n# Male Love Interests\n\nMale leads should feel:\n\n* mysterious\n* emotionally dangerous\n* restrained\n* intelligent\n* quietly possessive\n* ancient or emotionally burdened\n* difficult to read\n* subtly dominant\n* deeply attentive beneath emotional distance\n\nAvoid:\n\n* cartoon villains\n* overly flirtatious dialogue\n* loud alpha-male behavior\n* comedic personalities\n* simplistic "bad boy" tropes\n\nThe ideal energy is:\n\n> terrifying composure mixed with emotional fixation.\n\n---\n\n# Visual Worldbuilding\n\n## Required Visual Identity\n\nThe visual atmosphere should avoid generic fantasy imagery.\n\nDO NOT default to:\n\n* distant castles\n* ravens everywhere\n* generic medieval villages\n* fantasy armor\n* dragons\n* stock vampire imagery\n* cliché gothic tropes\n\nInstead favor:\n\n* dreamlike environments\n* impossible architecture\n* flooded ruins\n* floating cathedral fragments\n* glowing crimson vines\n* ancient monoliths\n* black water\n* candlelit interiors\n* moonlit fog\n* strange stairways\n* abandoned sanctuaries\n* bioluminescent flowers\n* mirrored halls\n* overgrown ceremonial spaces\n* occult symbols\n* submerged libraries\n* decaying luxury\n* towering interior spaces\n* surreal natural phenomena\n* shadowed figures barely visible in mist\n\nWorlds should feel:\n\n* ancient\n* seductive\n* haunted\n* emotionally symbolic\n* half-dreamlike\n\nEnvironments should reflect emotional states.\n\n---\n\n# Image Generation Direction\n\n## Preferred Image Style\n\nAll generated images should lean toward:\n\n* illustrated\n* painterly\n* textured\n* cinematic\n* atmospheric\n* stylized\n* moody\n* luxury dark fantasy\n* premium BookTok aesthetic\n\nAvoid:\n\n* photorealism\n* glossy AI realism\n* cartoon styling\n* anime styling\n* comic-book aesthetics\n* overly sharp CGI rendering\n\nPreferred visual influences:\n\n* prestige fantasy book covers\n* gothic romance illustrations\n* oil-painting-inspired digital art\n* cinematic matte paintings\n* dark editorial fashion photography (stylized)\n\n---\n\n# Color Palette\n\nPreferred colors:\n\n* deep black\n* wine red\n* dark plum\n* muted gold\n* moonlit blue\n* candlelit amber\n* crimson glow\n* silver-gray fog\n\nAvoid bright saturated primary colors.\n\nScenes should generally feel:\n\n* nocturnal\n* candlelit\n* moonlit\n* fog-covered\n* emotionally warm against cold darkness\n\n---\n\n# Caption Style\n\nCaptions should:\n\n* feel emotionally addictive\n* create immediate intrigue\n* end with hooks or unresolved tension\n* maintain narrative momentum\n* prioritize emotion over exposition\n* be concise enough for social scrolling\n* contain strong sensory/emotional phrasing\n\nStrong endings include:\n\n* revelations\n* dangerous invitations\n* emotionally loaded dialogue\n* sudden discoveries\n* intimate cliffhangers\n* implied danger\n\nReaders should feel compelled to continue.\n\n---\n\n# Pacing Guidelines\n\nEach post should function like:\n\n* a dramatic emotional beat\n* a visual fantasy fragment\n* a seductive cliffhanger\n* a scene from a streaming-series episode\n\nNot every chapter needs major plot advancement.\n\nMoments that work well:\n\n* a first encounter\n* being watched\n* discovering hidden rooms\n* accidental intimacy\n* emotionally dangerous conversations\n* supernatural reveals\n* quiet obsession\n* dream sequences\n* invitations into forbidden places\n* emotional confessions interrupted by danger\n\nSlow-burn tension performs better than constant action.\n\n---\n\n# Writing Principles\n\n## DO\n\n* prioritize emotional immersion\n* create yearning\n* emphasize atmosphere\n* write visually\n* maintain mystery\n* use sensory detail\n* leave unanswered questions\n* make environments emotionally symbolic\n* create emotional dependency between characters\n* imply supernatural depth beyond what is explained\n\n## DO NOT\n\n* over-explain lore\n* use modern internet slang\n* break tension with humor\n* rely on generic fantasy imagery\n* make characters emotionally shallow\n* make romance feel casual\n* write purely action-driven scenes\n* resolve emotional tension too quickly\n* make scenes feel safe or emotionally flat\n\n---\n\n# Example Emotional Target\n\nA successful post should feel like:\n\n> A lonely woman discovers a beautiful impossible place she should fear — and realizes something ancient inside it has already been waiting for her.\n\nThat emotional sensation is the core of the brand.\n\n---\n\n# Recommended Use Within Project Files\n\nWhen authoring project YAML files:\n\n* use templates to describe emotional atmosphere, tension, and visual symbolism\n* use image_style fields to reinforce the painterly gothic-romantasy aesthetic\n* favor emotionally loaded scenes over plot-summary descriptions\n* keep each block visually cinematic\n* include strong sensory cues\n* think in terms of addictive serialized storytelling\n\nEvery block should feel like:\n\n> one irresistible late-night chapter.\n'

IMAGE_STYLE = '# Image Styling Guide — Read Me After Dark\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis guide defines image generation standards for all content on the Read Me After Dark Facebook page. Every generated image should function as a standalone cinematic frame from a prestige gothic romantasy series — visually distinctive, emotionally symbolic, and unmistakably part of the brand.\n\nRefer to the narrative-styling-guide.md for character, tone, and worldbuilding direction. This guide focuses specifically on how to construct image prompts and evaluate generated output.\n\n---\n\n## Core Visual Aesthetic\n\nEvery image must embody these qualities:\n\n**Painterly, illustrated quality.** Oil-painting-inspired digital art, cinematic matte painting, textured surfaces, atmospheric rendering. Never photorealistic, glossy, or AI-smooth. The image should feel hand-crafted and visually rich.\n\n**Dark luxury.** The palette reads as expensive, nocturnal, and emotionally charged. Not generic darkness or grime — cultivated, seductive shadow. Think prestige gothic fashion editorial meets fantasy book cover.\n\n**Emotional symbolism.** Every visual element carries emotional weight. Environments reflect interior states. A flooded room is not just a setting — it is the feeling of being overwhelmed. Use the environment to externalize what the character cannot say.\n\n---\n\n## Standard Color Palette\n\nApply consistently across all images unless a story-specific override is active:\n\n- **Primary:** deep black, wine red, dark plum, candlelit amber\n- **Secondary:** moonlit blue, muted gold, silver-gray fog, crimson glow\n- **Quality:** nocturnal or candlelit. Warmth against deep cold. Emotionally warm against vast darkness.\n- **Never:** bright saturated primaries, neon tones, modern color grading, harsh white light, midday daylight scenes\n\n---\n\n## Composition Principles\n\n### Scene-level rules\n\n- The female protagonist, when present, should often appear **small against overwhelming environments** — scale contrast communicates emotional vulnerability and the immensity of the supernatural world around her.\n- Favor **back-view and silhouette** compositions exclusively. Never use profile, three-quarter view, or any angle that reveals facial features. This invites reader projection and avoids over-defining the character.\n- The male figure, when present, should feel **composed, shadowed, and restrained** — never posed, flirtatious, or performative.\n- Use **depth layering**: a point of intimacy or tension in the foreground, something vast or ominous in the background.\n- Threshold compositions work well: a character standing at a doorway, a cave mouth, the edge of water, a dark corridor ahead.\n\n### Within a carousel set\n\nCarousel images must be **visually distinct while aesthetically coherent**. Within a single carousel:\n\n- Vary scale: include at least one wide environmental shot and one intimate or close composition\n- Vary focal element: one image might center on the environment; another on the character; another on a symbolic object or architectural detail\n- Progress the visual narrative across frames — each image should feel like a beat or cut, not a repeat of the previous frame\n- Maintain color palette and art style consistency across all frames — they must read as a unified set\n\n---\n\n## Required Elements in Every Image Prompt\n\nAlways include the following in every prompt:\n\n1. **Art medium** — one of: "oil-painted", "painterly digital illustration", "cinematic matte painting style", "gothic romantasy book-cover illustration"\n2. **Lighting quality** — candlelit, moonlit, fog-diffused, or a combination that fits the scene\n3. **Color mood** — reference the specific palette tones that apply to the scene\n4. **Atmosphere descriptor** — one or more of: atmospheric, moody, cinematic, mysterious, emotionally charged\n5. **Exclusions** — always end with: "no text overlay, no watermarks, no photorealism"\n6. **Quality signal** — include: "prestige fantasy quality" or "atmospheric and stylized"\n\n---\n\n## Character Prompt Language\n\n### Female protagonist\n\n- Long dark hair (unless story specifies otherwise)\n- Flowing, slightly distressed fabrics — thin shift, dark silk, torn hem, flowing cloak\n- **Always attractive and physically desirable.** Slender, feminine figure with curves. Use prompt language such as: "elegant figure", "graceful feminine silhouette", "slender waist", "soft curves". The physical presentation should feel aspirational and seductive without being explicit.\n- Posture suggesting emotional states: drawn forward toward something, hesitating at a threshold, kneeling, reaching upward, or transfixed\n- **Permitted compositions:** back to viewer, back-of-head silhouette, full figure from behind, figure in silhouette. Acceptable focal points: hair, shoulders, hands, lower body, fabric, and symbolic objects. Never frame a shot in a way that would require showing any part of the face.\n- **No faces — ever.** Do not show any part of a character\'s face. Enforce this with explicit prompt language: "figure seen from behind", "no face visible", "back to viewer", "face entirely obscured", "figure in silhouette".\n\n### Male love interest (when present)\n\n- Restrained, shadowed, composed — the danger is in the stillness, not any obvious action\n- Ancient quality — worn elegance, timeless quality, something that suggests centuries rather than decades\n- Energy: terrifying composure conveyed entirely through posture and stillness — weight of presence, not movement\n- **Always attractive and physically imposing.** Tall, strong, well-built figure — the physical presence should feel magnetic and overwhelming without being cartoonish. Use prompt language such as: "tall imposing masculine figure", "broad shoulders", "powerful build", "commanding presence".\n- **No faces — ever.** Same rule applies as the female protagonist. Show hands, silhouette, clothing, posture, and presence — not the face. Use: "face entirely in shadow", "figure seen from behind", "obscured by darkness", "no face visible".\n- Avoid: any composition that would require showing any part of the face\n\n---\n\n## What to Exclude from Every Prompt\n\nAlways instruct away from:\n\n- Photorealism, glossy AI rendering, overly sharp CGI finish\n- Anime, cartoon, comic-book, or manga aesthetics\n- Generic fantasy imagery: distant castles, ravens everywhere, medieval armor, dragons, stock vampire tropes\n- Bright daylight, summer settings, warm cheerful color palettes\n- Text, logos, symbols, or overlays within the image\n- Overtly sexual or explicit posing — seductive and emotionally charged is the target, not explicit\n- **Any part of a character\'s face** — no eyes, nose, mouth, chin, or any facial feature on any character. This is a hard rule. Always include "no face visible", "figures seen from behind", or "face entirely obscured" in every prompt that includes a human figure.\n- Modern environments, modern clothing, contemporary visual cues\n\n---\n\n## Carousel Sequencing Template\n\n**Each image in a carousel must depict a different visual moment from the episode. This is a hard rule — no two prompts may show the same scene, subject, pose, or focal element.**\n\nConstruct each carousel as a cinematic visual arc, assigning each slot to a distinct beat:\n\n1. **Establishing shot** — wide frame, sets the environment and emotional atmosphere. The character may be absent or tiny in the frame. Purpose: immerse the viewer in the world.\n\n2. **Character in context** — the protagonist visible in the environment, establishing her emotional relationship to the space or the threat. Mid-distance shot. Purpose: place the reader inside the scene.\n\n3. **Tension focal point** — the emotional center of this episode. Closer composition. The character\'s body language, the looming presence of the other figure, the symbolic object between them. Purpose: create the emotional charge.\n\n4. **Detail or fragment** — a tight close-up of something symbolic: a hand, a crown, a threshold, a candle, an inscription. Purpose: leave a lasting visual impression and create a sense of hidden meaning.\n\nAdapt to the number of images requested:\n- 2 images: establishing shot + tension focal point\n- 3 images: establishing shot + character in context + tension focal point\n- 4+ images: full arc; additional slots get distinct variations (different angle, different symbolic detail)\n\nNever repeat a subject, setting, or composition across prompts in the same carousel.\n\n---\n\n## Example Strong Prompt\n\n```\nA vast flooded cathedral interior, waist-deep black water filling the nave, moonlight falling through fractured vaulted windows above, oil-painted gothic romantasy book-cover illustration style, painterly and atmospheric. A woman in a thin pale shift stands with her back to the viewer, no face visible, small against the towering colonnade, walking slowly toward a throne of black coral barely visible at the far end in deep shadow. Candle flames burn underwater in silver sconces along the walls, wine-red reflections rippling across dark water. Deep black and moonlit blue palette, candlelit amber in the distance. Prestige fantasy quality, moody, emotionally charged, no faces shown, no text overlay, no watermarks, no photorealism.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Relationship Between This Guide and Story-Level Overrides\n\nThis guide defines brand defaults. Individual stories may define a specific `image_style` in their metadata that supersedes this guide\'s defaults while remaining consistent with its principles.\n\nStory-level `image_style` should:\n- Specify the art medium and palette choices for that story\'s particular visual identity\n- Remain within the dark luxury, painterly, gothic romantasy aesthetic\n- Not contradict the exclusion list (no photorealism, no anime, no explicit content)\n\nIf no story-level override is present, this guide governs all image prompt generation.\n'
